Lift Station 1 is one of two community lift stations servicing the Binche Whut&apos;en community&nbsp;on IR2. The lift station is located at the intersection between Binche Keyoh Way, Mandine Drive, and Duncan&nbsp;Drive.&nbsp; A&nbsp;condition assessment of the existing lift stations was completed as part of emergency operations&nbsp;and maintenance work approved by Indigenous Services Canada.&nbsp; It was&nbsp;recommended that the wet well of Lift Station 1 be replaced and relocated across Duncan Drive prior to the&nbsp;construction of a back up generator and electrical upgrades.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The Nation&#39;s consultant conducted a review to identify potential environmental effects&nbsp;associated with the proposed works on reserve land and confirm compliance with the Impact Assessment Act.&nbsp;&nbsp;The review determined that no significant environmental impacts are anticipated with the implementation of&nbsp;standard mitigation measures.&nbsp; The project is located within a previously disturbed area and will require only&nbsp;minimal vegetation clearing.&nbsp; Additionally, the proposed culvert installation within existing roadside drainage&nbsp;ditches is not expected to trigger a Fisheries Act Authorization or a BC Water Sustainability Act Section 11&nbsp;Notification or Approval, as the ditches are man-made features with intermittent flow and limited capacity to&nbsp;support fish or fish habitat.&nbsp;&nbsp;A Species at Risk screening identified several species with potential to occur in the broader area.&nbsp; A BC&nbsp;Conservation Data Centre search also identified a mapped occurrence of White Sturgeon in Stuart Lake; however, no project-related impacts are anticipated to any of these species if the project follows appropriate mitigation&nbsp;measures.&nbsp; Mitigation measures have been proposed as part of the submission to Indigenous Services Canada.&nbsp;&nbsp;Finally, an archaeological records search through the ILRR Archaeology Branch identified no sensitive&nbsp;archaeological sites within the project area. As the project is located within IR2, engagement with a qualified&nbsp;archaeologist and implementation of a chance-find procedure during construction is recommended.

&nbsp;

The lift station project includes a prefabricated lift station wet well design, and structural pad designed based on the geotechnical conditions at the proposed site.&nbsp;&nbsp;Once the prefabricated lift station is installed, a 300 mm by 500 mm tall upstand wall is recommended around&nbsp;the base of the wet well.&nbsp; Within the lift station, unreinforced mass concrete is to be poured under the base of the&nbsp;tank separately as required by tank supplier.

&nbsp;

Proposed Project: North Cargo Hardstands&nbsp;

The North Cargo Hardstands Project forms part of the National Trade Corridors Fund (NTCF) funding agreement between the Greater Toronto Airports Authority (GTAA) and Transport Canada. The primary&nbsp;objective&nbsp;of this initiative is to enhance air cargo capacity at Toronto Pearson International Airport.&nbsp;

The proposed development will be&nbsp;located&nbsp;airside near the east end of Runway 05-23, southwest of the intersection of Airport Road and Derry Road, on previously developed land. The facility will support increased&nbsp;aircraft&nbsp;cargo operations and parking capacity. The project scope includes:&nbsp;


	Construction of approximately 120,000 square metres of pavement for&nbsp;aircraft&nbsp;parking and manoeuvring&nbsp;&nbsp;
	Installation of high-mast lighting systems&nbsp;&nbsp;
	Provision of hydrant&nbsp;fueling&nbsp;infrastructure and&nbsp;aircraft&nbsp;servicing equipment&nbsp;&nbsp;
	Development of a new connection to existing Taxiway Juliet&nbsp;&nbsp;
	Implementation of new drainage infrastructure, including a stormwater retention system and oil/water interceptors to manage both quantity and quality of runoff&nbsp;&nbsp;


Construction activities will involve excavation, clearing and grubbing, utility relocation and removal, site grading, installation of underground services, paving, line marking, lighting installation, and concrete works, along with supporting boarding infrastructure.&nbsp;

Sustainability best practices will be integrated into both the design and construction phases, aligning with the broader Long-Term Investment in Facilities and Terminals (LIFT) Program at Toronto Pearson Airport.&nbsp;

Note: The six cargo&nbsp;aircraft&nbsp;stands included in this project form part of GTAA&#39;s broader LIFT Program, which encompasses a total of 10&nbsp;aircraft&nbsp;stands. The remaining stands will be addressed under a separate project posting&nbsp;(Reference # 90410).&nbsp;

The Proposed Project:&nbsp;Parking Lot Repaving at Toronto Pearson International Airport&nbsp;

This project involves the following:&nbsp;

As part of the Greater Toronto Airports Authority&#39;s (GTAA&#39;s) Toronto Pearson Airport Longterm Investment in Facilities &amp; Terminals (LIFT) Program, the repaving of a parking lot will be&nbsp;required. The existing employee parking lot is&nbsp;located&nbsp;at 2498 Britannia Road East at Toronto Pearson International Airport.&nbsp;&nbsp;

The project scope includes:&nbsp;

The removal of the existing asphalt on a section of the parking lot with repaving of new asphalt, resetting storm drains (as&nbsp;required), repainting lines, and other parking&nbsp;lot&nbsp;rehabilitation (as&nbsp;required).&nbsp;Sustainability best practices will be integrated into both the design and construction phases, aligning with the broader Long-Term Investment in Facilities and Terminals (LIFT) Program at Toronto Pearson Airport.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The Lorneville Small Craft Harbour (SCH) is situated on a natural promontory extending northward into Lorneville Harbour.&nbsp; The harbour is further protected by a 165 m-long rubblemound breakwater (SCH Facility Number (#) 301) that extends out from the headland.&nbsp; The existing public wharf (#401) is a tee-shaped timber pile structure located on the lee side of the breakwater.&nbsp; The structure consists of square-post trestle bents along the approach stem and a timber pile bent system at the tee section.&nbsp; The wharf is more than 60 years old and has reached the end of its service life.

&nbsp;

The proposed works will involve the complete demolition and removal of the wharf structure.&nbsp; Demolition activities will likely be undertaken using land-based construction methods, either from a temporary earthen platform constructed along the interior side of the existing breakwater (#301) or by installation of a temporary trestle bridge.&nbsp; These approaches will facilitate the use of land-based equipment, including long-reach excavators, cranes, and haul trucks operating in dry conditions.&nbsp; Alternatively, the methodology could also involve a barge operation with equipment such as a crane and/or excavator operating from the floating platform to dismantle the wharf and relay material to the upland.

&nbsp;

The existing structure will likely be dismantled in sections or segments and temporarily placed along the approach road for further processing and separation.&nbsp; Materials suitable for recycling or reuse will be salvaged where practical, while unsuitable materials will be managed as waste, loaded onto trucks, and transported to provincially-approved disposal facilities.&nbsp; Demolition activities will also involve the removal of ballast boxes and stone and may also require incidental excavation of underwater sediments.&nbsp; Following completion of demolition activities, all temporary work areas and disturbed ground surfaces will be stabilized as required, with ballast and earthen materials/rock from temporary access reworked into the existing breakwater.&nbsp; All construction equipment and construction waste materials will be removed from the site.

&nbsp;

Demolition activities are anticipated to commence in November 2026 and be completed by the end of March 2027.&nbsp; Project commencement is, however, subject to DFO-SCH operational priorities and funding, as well as the completion of regulatory approval processes, therefore work may be delayed.&nbsp; The scope of the environmental effects determination (subject to S.82 of the Impact Assessment Act) will, therefore, be based on the works being completed over a period of three (3) months commencing within a period of 5 years following the determination of significance of environmental effects.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The Lorneville SCH is a fishing harbour that was originally developed to serve the fishing industry in the area.&nbsp; The site has been used as a fishing harbour for several decades. Departmental records indicate that a breakwater has been in place since at least the late 1800s.&nbsp; Its well-protected location provides easy access to the fishing grounds of the Bay of Fundy.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Given that the existing wharf is well beyond its service life and load restricted, the status quo of leaving the deteriorated structures available and in service represents a contingent liability to the Crown.&nbsp; Repairs are not practical and complete reconstruction would be necessary to provide the same level of service.

&nbsp;

The four (4) homeport vessels at the Lorneville SCH have relocated to the Five Fathom Harbour SCH (approximately 8.5 kms to the northwest) due to the harbour&#39;s deteriorated condition.&nbsp; Following review of the costs associated with reconstruction of the wharf at Lorneville, DFO-SCH decided that permanent accommodation of these users at Five Fathom Harbour SCH through the installation of an additional floating dock was the most practicable and cost-effective long-term option.
